# Backlog & content safety

Check `docs/backlog.md` before starting any new work — it lists agreed-but-unbuilt items so work isn't duplicated or invented.

## Known open items (do these before new feature work)

1. **Build-time frontmatter validation** — loaders currently use TypeScript assertions that validate nothing at runtime. Plan: add `zod`, define schemas mirroring `ProjectFrontmatter` / `ArticleFrontmatter` (prefer `z.infer` so types stay single-source), validate at load time in `lib/content/`, and make malformed frontmatter fail `pnpm run build` loudly with the offending slug + field.
2. **SEO/discovery** — sitemap.ts, robots.ts, canonical URLs (needs metadataBase), per-project/article OG images, RSS. Use the installed Next.js docs in `node_modules/next/dist/docs/` for file-convention syntax.

## Content safety rules (always enforce)

- Frontmatter must never appear in rendered output. It is stripped by remark-frontmatter in next.config.ts and parsed separately by gray-matter in lib/content. Visible frontmatter = build bug; fix the pipeline, never CSS-hide it.
- No placeholder URLs (`repoUrl: "https://github.com"` with no repo path). Omit rather than fake.
- No unmeasured metrics. If a number wasn't measured, don't render it.
- Loader casts like `frontmatter as SomeType` are assertions, not validation — don't add new ones; prefer Zod schemas once introduced.

Any deviation from these rules or from docs/backlog.md plans must be logged as a one-line entry in docs/decisions.md in the same change.
